  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(53.998pt, 84.001pt, "basis for future studies on selecting repump and off-resonant excitation wavelengths that minimize unwanted ")
  #line-at(53.998pt, 100.443pt, "ionization, thereby informing operational conditions for achieving charge-stable silicon-vacancy-based spin–photon ")
  #line-at(54.009pt, 116.758pt, "interfaces. ")
  #line-at(54.009pt, 149.642pt, "We now consider the optimal excitation conditions for PDMR of the V2 center. The fundamental noise floor arises ")
  #line-at(54.009pt, 165.957pt, "from the combined contributions of amplifier noise and photocurrent shot noise. At the same time, the laser power ")
  #line-at(53.998pt, 182.399pt, "fluctuations add a further noise component, which has previously been suggested as the dominant noise source")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 6.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(531.84pt, 178.92pt, "25")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(538.68pt, 182.4pt, ". ")
  #line-at(53.997pt, 198.842pt, "Because both contributions scale with the total photocurrent, through its square root for shot noise and linearly for ")
  #line-at(53.997pt, 215.157pt, "laser-induced noise, reducing the background photocurrent is essential for improving the achievable sensitivity. ")
  #line-at(54.008pt, 248.041pt, "To quantify the relative contribution of the defect signal, we define the V2 photocurrent fraction (PCF) as ")
  #line-at(54.008pt, 264.356pt, "V2/(V2+BG) (Fig. 4a right), which provides a photocurrent-derived measure of the single-defect PDMR performance. ")
  #line-at(54.018pt, 280.798pt, "At high laser powers (8 mW), which are important for PDMR, the optimal excitation wavelength appears in the range ")
  #line-at(54.018pt, 297.24pt, "of 852–920 nm, owing to the suppression of background-defect ionization. However, the PCF in the present sample ")
  #line-at(54.029pt, 313.555pt, "is below 0.5, indicating that more than half of the photocurrent originates from the background defects rather than ")
  #line-at(54.029pt, 329.997pt, "from the V2 center. This suggests that the presence of background photocurrents, primarily from carbon vacancies, ")
  #line-at(54.029pt, 346.439pt, "increases the noise budget. ")
  #line-at(54.039pt, 379.196pt, "Because carbon vacancies are thermally more stable than silicon vacancies")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 6.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(368.28pt, 375.72pt, "41,42")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(384pt, 379.2pt, ", they are difficult to remove after V2 ")
  #line-at(54pt, 395.642pt, "formation. Nevertheless, carbon-vacancy densities can be reduced below 10")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 6.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(373.44pt, 392.16pt, "11")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(380.16pt, 395.64pt, " cm")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 6.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(395.64pt, 392.16pt, "−3")
  #line-at(405.72pt, 392.16pt, "43–45")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(423.12pt, 395.64pt, " by annealing under carbon-")
  #line-at(53.995pt, 411.955pt, "interstitial-rich conditions, which can be prepared using established techniques such as carbon-ion implantation")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 6.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(523.08pt, 408.48pt, "43")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(530.04pt, 411.96pt, " or ")
  #line-at(53.995pt, 428.402pt, "thermal oxidation")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 6.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(128.64pt, 424.92pt, "44,45")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(144.36pt, 428.4pt, ". These processes serve as effective pretreatments prior to silicon-vacancy creation, benefiting ")
  #line-at(54.009pt, 444.842pt, "both PDMR and spin–photon interface applications. Furthermore, using lower electron fluence suppresses the ")
  #line-at(54.009pt, 461.157pt, "absolute density of carbon vacancies, thereby reducing background photocurrent. Alternatively, irradiation schemes ")
  #line-at(54.009pt, 477.599pt, "dominated by nuclear stopping, such as proton or light-ion implantation, can improve the silicon vacancy/carbon ")
  #line-at(54.009pt, 494.041pt, "vacancy ratio (e.g., 1:1.5–1.7 reported by proton")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 6.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(266.04pt, 490.56pt, "46")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(273pt, 494.04pt, "). Although optimization of the electron fluence, implantation ")
  #line-at(53.996pt, 510.355pt, "parameters, defect-generation yield, and annealing temperature is required, these approaches represent promising ")
  #line-at(54.007pt, 526.797pt, "routes to suppress background photocurrent and enhance single-defect PDMR sensitivity. ")
  #line-at(54.017pt, 559.554pt, "While the optimization strategies discussed above directly address single-defect PDMR, the situation differs in ")
  #line-at(54.017pt, 575.996pt, "ensemble systems. A primary distinction is that photocurrent from V1 centers also contributes to the total spin-")
  #line-at(54.017pt, 592.438pt, "insensitive signal. Recently, partially selective generation of V2 centers over V1 centers has been demonstrated using ")
  #line-at(54.017pt, 608.753pt, "focused-ion-beam irradiation from the ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "italic", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(225.638pt, 608.753pt, "m")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(233.305pt, 608.753pt, "-face rather than the conventional ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "italic", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(387.363pt, 608.753pt, "c")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(392.075pt, 608.753pt, "-face.")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 6.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(415.56pt, 605.28pt, "47")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(422.52pt, 608.76pt, " Although this method can ")
  #line-at(54.008pt, 625.202pt, "potentially contribute to the improvement of the spin contrast of ensemble V2 PDMR, ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "italic", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(424.811pt, 625.202pt, "m")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(432.489pt, 625.202pt, "-face wafers are currently ")
  #line-at(54.018pt, 641.644pt, "not scalable for electronic integration. A more practical strategy, motivated by our findings, is to suppress the ")
  #line-at(54.018pt, 657.959pt, "photoionization of V1 centers. ")
  #line-at(54.018pt, 690.843pt, "Although ensemble measurements cannot separate V1 and background contributions, our single-defect data allow us ")
  #line-at(54.018pt, 707.158pt, "to infer their expected ensemble behavior. As a performance indicator of ensemble PDMR based on photocurrent ")
  #line-at(54.029pt, 723.6pt, "data, we consider V2/(V2+V1) and V2/(V2+V1+BG) as PCFs (Fig. 4b). These metrics assume that the densities of ")
  #line-at(54.05pt, 740.042pt, "V1 and V2 centers are comparable and that the background defect density scales similarly to that of V2 centers with ")
  #line-at(54.05pt, 756.357pt, "irradiation dose. ")
]

// arXiv source page 9
#original-page(595.32pt, 841.92pt, none)[
  #set text(font: "YuMincho-Regular", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(294.84pt, 785.28pt, "9 ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(53.998pt, 100.443pt, "A higher PCF is obtained at longer wavelengths under anti-Stokes conditions for both V1 and V2 centers. Although ")
  #line-at(54.009pt, 116.758pt, "the ionization cross-sections of V1 and V2 are similar, the inefficient excitation of V1 at wavelengths far longer than ")
  #line-at(54.019pt, 133.2pt, "its ZPL suppresses its photocurrent contribution. While an ODMR study reported improved contrast by suppressing ")
  #line-at(54.009pt, 149.642pt, "V1 excitation at wavelengths above 850 nm")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 6.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(240.6pt, 146.16pt, "10")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(247.56pt, 149.64pt, ", our photocurrent measurements show that even longer wavelengths, ")
  #line-at(53.995pt, 165.955pt, "above 900 nm, are required to suppress V1-origin photocurrent (Fig. 4b, left). This difference arises because high ")
  #line-at(53.995pt, 182.397pt, "laser powers typically used for PDMR can still populate the V1 excited state even at wavelengths slightly above its ")
  #line-at(53.985pt, 198.839pt, "ZPL, enabling ionization and photocurrent generation. At the same time, the photocurrent from the background defect ")
  #line-at(53.985pt, 215.154pt, "is likewise reduced at longer wavelengths, improving the PCF (Fig. 4b right). Consequently, within the wavelength ")
  #line-at(53.985pt, 231.596pt, "range examined up to 940 nm, longer-wavelength excitation yields higher PCF and is therefore advantageous for ")
  #line-at(53.974pt, 248.038pt, "ensemble PDMR.  ")
  #line-at(53.974pt, 280.795pt, "Beyond V1, V2, and carbon vacancies, additional defects may contribute to the background current in ensemble-")
  #line-at(53.974pt, 297.237pt, "defect PDMR. Photocurrent-only features visible in our maps (Fig. 1d and Ref. 25) likely originate from non-emissive ")
  #line-at(53.985pt, 313.552pt, "or near-infrared emitters outside the detection range and generate photocurrents comparable to silicon vacancies. ")
  #line-at(53.985pt, 329.994pt, "Their characterization lies beyond the scope of this work, but they represent an important direction for improving ")
  #line-at(53.985pt, 346.436pt, "ensemble PDMR. ")
  #line-at(53.985pt, 379.193pt, "In summary, photocurrent spectroscopy of individual silicon vacancies reveals that the photoionization cross-sections ")
  #line-at(53.995pt, 395.635pt, "of V1 and V2 are comparable and increase toward shorter wavelengths. We also find that carbon vacancies dominate ")
  #line-at(53.995pt, 411.95pt, "the background photocurrent, affecting the charge environment. These results show that V2 is intrinsically more ")
  #line-at(53.995pt, 428.392pt, "robust against ionization under resonant excitation than V1 and provide quantitative information for optimizing ")
  #line-at(53.995pt, 444.834pt, "repump conditions in spin–photon interface applications. For PDMR, we identify an optimal excitation wavelength ")
  #line-at(53.995pt, 461.149pt, "range of 852–920 nm for single V2 centers and a longer wavelength (~940 nm in the range studied) for ensembles, ")
  #line-at(54.006pt, 477.591pt, "and show that reducing carbon-vacancy density can further improve spin-readout SNR. Our technique is compatible ")
  #line-at(54.006pt, 494.033pt, "with recently developed defects-in-diode structures")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 6.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(269.16pt, 490.56pt, "20–22 ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(288.36pt, 494.04pt, "to stabilize and tune optical transitions, and the combination ")
  #line-at(54.002pt, 510.355pt, "of electrical and optical probing would enable access to charge-dynamic processes that remain invisible in purely ")
  #line-at(54.002pt, 526.797pt, "optical methods. This work deepens the understanding of charge stability in spin–photon interface applications and ")
  #line-at(54.002pt, 543.239pt, "provides quantitative guidelines for improving PDMR efficiency, thereby advancing scalable optical and electrical ")
  #line-at(54.002pt, 559.554pt, "quantum-device platforms in silicon carbide. ")
]

